;; Documentation, input ports and named capabilities. No model instantiation.
(define role-cell "You are constructing executable Lain networks in a live session. Produce complete raw Lain forms only, no prose or Markdown. Source is admitted into the current child. Unknown cells wait for information; definitions and calls build dataflow, not an imperative execution sequence. Instructions and tools are entirely in this environment.")
(define language-introduction-cell "(define f (network [x rate] (* x rate))) defines a reusable callable; (if (>= x y) x y) chooses the larger numeric input; (f 4 3) computes 12. (+ a b), (- a b), (* a b), (/ a b) compute connected numeric results. (str \"Total: \" answer) constructs connected text. (-> answer output) connects an existing output; do not redefine parent ports. (cell:reference f) returns an edge reference; (cell:value reference) observes its strongest value, including a callable; ((cell:value reference) 4 3) invokes that callable. (seq:of a b) builds a vector of available values. (data:get :key record) reads a field. (data:read-edn \"{:a 1}\") returns {:status :parsed :value {:a 1}}. (inspect:cell (cell:reference answer)) inspects immediate value/input/output connections. Documentation examples are not installed networks.")
(define catalog-guide "catalog:register takes an EDN metadata string and a cell reference, and returns a receipt. Example: (catalog:register \"{:catalog/id :example/v1 :catalog/description \\\"Multiply quantity by rate\\\" :catalog/interface \\\"[quantity rate] -> amount\\\" :catalog/assumptions \\\"Compatible units\\\" :catalog/status :current :catalog/tag [:linear] :catalog/capability [[:catalog/id :cap/linear]]}\" (cell:reference f)). Use normal escaped string quotes in actual source. Registration records model claims, not proof of correctness. Repeating identical registration is idempotent; conflicting identities are rejected. catalog:resolve takes a registered keyword id and returns its LIVE CELL REFERENCE; example (define candidate (cell:value (catalog:resolve :example/v1))) then (candidate 4 3). Database persistence does not restore executable closures after restart. catalog:query takes EDN query text and EDN rules text and returns rows. Always use :in $ %. Example: (catalog:query \"[:find ?id :in $ % :where [?n :catalog/id ?id] [?n :catalog/status :current]]\" \"[]\"). Only relation queries, fact clauses and named rule calls are needed. No eval or host code. Results have at most 64 rows. Search and inspect applicability before constructing a duplicate; you may reject unsuitable candidates. Exact database schema (Datomic Local): :catalog/id is a unique keyword identity. :catalog/description, :catalog/interface and :catalog/assumptions are strings. :catalog/status is a keyword such as :current or :superseded. :catalog/tag is a many-valued keyword attribute. :catalog/capability and :catalog/depends-on are many-valued ENTITY REFERENCES, not keyword values. For example, [?root :catalog/id :cap/event-cost] binds the internal entity id to ?root; [?root :catalog/depends-on ?next] binds another internal entity id. To obtain its public keyword, join [?next :catalog/id ?next-id]. Registration lookup refs like [:catalog/id :cap/linear] resolve to those internal entity ids. All registered candidates have description/interface/assumptions/status. Do not guess alternate storage shapes. The supported subset is :find variables :in $ % :where triples and named-rule calls. No or-join, arbitrary predicates, aggregates, pull, or host functions. A transitive relation operates on entity ids. Multiple rules with the same head provide alternatives. Query only the supplied schema.")
(define transitive-guide "Compose reasoning by joining on shared variables. A rule head names a relation; multiple bodies with that head are alternatives. A base rule establishes one edge; a recursive rule composes one edge with a shorter path. Example EDN rules: [[(requires ?a ?b) [?a :catalog/depends-on ?b]] [(requires ?a ?b) [?a :catalog/depends-on ?mid] (requires ?mid ?b)]]. These compute transitive dependency reachability, NOT transitive correctness. Use them with :in $ %. Query a capability reachable from a problem, then join [?operator :catalog/capability ?capability] and inspect status/interface/assumptions. Tags are discovery hints, not proof. Cycles should terminate under Datalog set semantics. The concept graph includes :cap/event-cost -> :cap/batch-cost -> :cap/linear, and :cap/event-cost -> :cap/percentage. No pricing formula is stored in this graph. Generic composition example (documentation, not installed data): with a concept :cap/example-task and rules requires above, use [:find ?id ?interface :in $ % :where [?root :catalog/id :cap/example-task] (requires ?root ?cap) [?operator :catalog/capability ?cap] [?operator :catalog/id ?id] [?operator :catalog/interface ?interface]]. There are TWO different relations: depends-on relates concept to prerequisite concept; capability relates an operator to its concept. Reachability returns concepts, not operators. The final capability join discovers operators. An empty result is an observation about your query, not proof that no suitable operator exists: inspect the relation directions and joins before concluding absence. Use descriptions and assumptions to assess applicability; current status is a claim rather than proof.")
(define build-task "Earlier tasks: implement a general quantity-times-unit-price callable, and a general callable that adds a fractional surcharge to an amount. Register these as :operator/linear-v1 and :operator/percentage-v1 with respective :cap/linear and :cap/percentage capabilities, :current status, precise interfaces and assumptions. Also implement an obsolete legacy quantity charge that captures a fixed unit price of 2, register :operator/legacy-v1 with :cap/linear and :superseded status, and implement a numeric maximum callable registered :operator/max-v1 with :cap/maximum and :current status. Choose your own Lain binding names. Keep all computations reusable. Bind each registration receipt, then connect (seq:of receipt1 receipt2 receipt3 receipt4) to library-ready so the next task waits for all registrations. Do not solve a later problem or publish its answer.")
(define search-task "Discover existing computational operators for this new problem: an event requires 120 printed pieces at 3 currency units each, plus 8 assembly hours at 15 per hour. Add an 8% service surcharge to the combined charge. Search the capability graph starting at :cap/event-cost with a recursive Datalog rule; include candidate ids, descriptions, interfaces, assumptions and statuses in result rows. Include obsolete candidates so you can explicitly assess their applicability. Connect the actual query result to discovery-results. This stage is discovery only; do not compute the answer or install pricing networks. No earlier source or generated operator names are supplied.")
(define solve-task "An event needs 120 pieces at 3 each and 8 assembly hours at 15 per hour, with an 8% surcharge on the combined amount. Solve using actual input cells quantity, unit-price, hours, hourly-rate, surcharge-rate. Inspect memory-cell query results, reject obsolete or incompatible interfaces, and reuse suitable retained callables via catalog:resolve and cell:value. Compose their results. Connect a computed numeric result to answer and text made from the computed result to user-output. Record your selection and rejection reasons as a string in selection-notes. Do not hardcode the numeric answer. The host will change quantity after completion and check propagation without any further model request.")
(define ask "An event requires 120 printed pieces at 3 currency units each, plus 8 assembly hours at 15 per hour. Add an 8% service surcharge to the combined charge. What is the total?")
(define library-ready)
(define discovery-results)
(define answer)
(define user-output)
(define selection-notes)
(define quantity)
(define unit-price)
(define hours)
(define hourly-rate)
(define surcharge-rate)
(define start)
(define add-binding (network [e name ref] (extend-env e (env:reference-slot name ref))))
(define seed (let [e0 (empty-env)
e1 (add-binding e0 "role-cell" (cell:reference role-cell))
e2 (add-binding e1 "language-introduction-cell" (cell:reference language-introduction-cell))
e3 (add-binding e2 "catalog-guide" (cell:reference catalog-guide))
e4 (add-binding e3 "transitive-guide" (cell:reference transitive-guide))
e5 e4
e6 e5
e7 e6
e8 e7
e9 (add-binding e8 "library-ready" (cell:reference library-ready))
e10 (add-binding e9 "discovery-results" (cell:reference discovery-results))
e11 (add-binding e10 "answer" (cell:reference answer))
e12 (add-binding e11 "user-output" (cell:reference user-output))
e13 (add-binding e12 "selection-notes" (cell:reference selection-notes))
e14 (add-binding e13 "quantity" (cell:reference quantity))
e15 (add-binding e14 "unit-price" (cell:reference unit-price))
e16 (add-binding e15 "hours" (cell:reference hours))
e17 (add-binding e16 "hourly-rate" (cell:reference hourly-rate))
e18 (add-binding e17 "surcharge-rate" (cell:reference surcharge-rate))
e19 (add-binding e18 "start" (cell:reference start))
e20 (add-binding e19 "+" (cell:reference +))
e21 (add-binding e20 "-" (cell:reference -))
e22 (add-binding e21 "*" (cell:reference *))
e23 (add-binding e22 "/" (cell:reference /))
e24 (add-binding e23 "str" (cell:reference str))
e25 (add-binding e24 ">=" (cell:reference >=))
e26 (add-binding e25 "seq:of" (cell:reference seq:of))
e27 (add-binding e26 "data:get" (cell:reference data:get))
e28 (add-binding e27 "data:read-edn" (cell:reference data:read-edn))
e29 (add-binding e28 "cell:reference" (cell:reference cell:reference))
e30 (add-binding e29 "cell:value" (cell:reference cell:value))
e31 (add-binding e30 "catalog:register" (cell:reference catalog:register))
e32 (add-binding e31 "catalog:query" (cell:reference catalog:query))
e33 (add-binding e32 "catalog:resolve" (cell:reference catalog:resolve))
e34 (add-binding e33 "inspect:cell" (cell:reference inspect:cell))
] e34))
